      "body": "## Purpose\r\n\r\n`stateless_init_torch_distributed_process_group` (`vllm/distributed/utils.py:576`) has two\r\narms: `gloo` goes to `init_gloo_process_group`, and every other backend is dispatched to\r\n`current_platform.stateless_init_device_torch_dist_pg`. `Platform` leaves that classmethod\r\nas `raise NotImplementedError` (`vllm/platforms/interface.py:1191`); `CudaPlatform`\r\n(in `cuda.py`) and `RocmPlatform` (in `rocm.py`) override it, and `XPUPlatform` does not.\r\n\r\nSo on XPU, any caller asking for a device-backend stateless group gets\r\n`NotImplementedError` — even though `XPUPlatform.dist_backend` already returns `\"xccl\"` and\r\n`torch.distributed.is_xccl_available()` is true on a normal XPU torch build. The in-tree\r\ncaller affected today is `vllm/distributed/stateless_coordinator.py:126`, which builds its\r\ndevice group with `backend=backend` (the CPU group next to it, line 135, is hardcoded\r\n`gloo` and is unaffected).\r\n\r\nThis PR implements the missing method for XPU with `ProcessGroupXCCL` and\r\n`ProcessGroup.BackendType.XCCL`, following `CudaPlatform`'s implementation step for step.\r\n\r\nScope notes:\r\n\r\n- **No behaviour changes on any existing path.** `ParallelConfig.stateless_init_dp_group`\r\n  (`vllm/config/parallel.py:685`) hardcodes `backend=\"gloo\"`, as do both fault-tolerance\r\n  sentinels (`vllm/v1/worker/sentinel/gpu_worker_sentinel.py:62`,\r\n  `vllm/v1/fault_tolerance/engine_core_sentinel.py:161`). None of them reach the platform\r\n  hook, so DP group formation is untouched. This only turns a raise into a working group\r\n  for callers that explicitly request the device backend.\r\n- Related history: #18763 introduced this hook, #22243 made DP group init\r\n  platform-dispatchable, and #31107 reported that a non-gloo implementation hung on a\r\n  CPU-based communication check. That check is no longer present in\r\n  `stateless_init_torch_distributed_process_group` on current `main`, and the DP path is\r\n  back to unconditional `gloo`, so neither hazard applies here.\r\n\r\n## Why this is not duplicating an existing PR\r\n\r\nSearched `vllm-project/vllm` for open PRs before writing this:\r\n\r\n```\r\ngh api \"search/issues?q=repo:vllm-project/vllm+is:pr+is:open+stateless_init_device_torch_dist_pg\"  # 0\r\ngh api \"search/issues?q=repo:vllm-project/vllm+is:pr+is:open+ProcessGroupXCCL\"                     # 1, unrelated\r\ngh api \"search/issues?q=repo:vllm-project/vllm+is:pr+xccl+stateless\"                               # 0, any state\r\n```\r\n\r\nNo open PR implements this method for any platform. #46210 (`add torchcomms backend for\r\nxpu support`) is the only other open PR editing `vllm/platforms/xpu.py`, but it changes\r\n`get_device_communicator_cls`; there is no textual or semantic overlap with this change.\r\n#53076 touches `xpu_worker.py`/`envs.py` only.\r\n\r\n## Test Plan\r\n\r\n```bash\r\npre-commit run --files vllm/platforms/xpu.py\r\n```\r\n\r\nFunctional verification is end-to-end rather than by unit test: a unit test for this method\r\nneeds two real XPU devices to complete the XCCL rendezvous, which no CI lane in this repo\r\ncurrently provides. Rather than add a test that would be skipped everywhere, the method was\r\nexercised through a stateless-process-group consumer on hardware — a TRL GRPO server-mode\r\nweight-transfer run, where a vLLM server process and a separate trainer process form an\r\nad-hoc 2-rank group over this code path.\r\n\r\n## Test Result\r\n\r\n- `pre-commit run --files vllm/platforms/xpu.py`: all hooks pass, including\r\n  `Run mypy for Python 3.10`, `Check SPDX headers`, and `Prevent new 'torch.cuda' APIs call`.\r\n- On 2x Intel Arc Pro B70 (`xccl`, TP=1, one card per process): the group forms, both ranks\r\n  log joining it, collectives complete against real tensors, and both processes exit\r\n  cleanly. Before this change the same path raised `NotImplementedError` at group\r\n  construction.\r\n\r\nNo model evaluation is included: this change adds a process-group constructor and does not\r\ntouch sampling, kernels, scheduling, or any code on a forward pass, so it cannot affect\r\nmodel output or accuracy. It is unreachable unless a caller explicitly requests a non-gloo\r\nstateless group.\r\n\r\n## Notes\r\n\r\nThis change is a requirement for a PR enabling XCCL weight transfer needed for reinforcement learning scenarios.\r\n\r\n🤖 Co-written with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\r\n",
